  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1014 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nOur coolest night may be Sunday into Monday morning. Guidance is\nhinting at some upper 40s possibly for lows in southern middle\nTennessee and portions of NE Alabama. Some patchy fog may be\npossible as well given lighter winds expected towards daybreak on\nMonday. \n\nA large deep layer cyclone off the New England coast will become an\nopen wave and begin ejecting northeast, allowing a trough axis to \ndrop southeast through the TN Valley on Monday. At this point, it\nappears to be a dry trough with no precipitation expected. A 5h ridge\nwill then shift east into the lower MS Valley and western Gulf of\nAmerica on Tuesday into Wednesday, and then further east into the\nSoutheast U.S. and southwest Atlantic basin by Thursday. Thus, a\nwarming trend is expected with highs that begin the week in the lower\nto middle 80s, warming into the upper 80s to around 90 during the \nmiddle part of the week. The medium range models then diverge on the\nhandling of a southwest U.S. upper low evolution. Even so, the low \nchances of showers or thunderstorms suggested by the NBM may be due \nmore to generalized moistening and destabilization due to increased \nsoutherly flow in advance of this trough. The ECMWF is stronger with \nthe ridge and delays the shift of this trough east, which may equate \nto a warmer scenario late next week.",
